{"kind":"assert","text":"Request eee72899's premise does not hold at current main. I measured it before working it, and the measurement contradicts the request I wrote.\n\nTHE CLAIM. \"ui/src/index.css line 3 is a bare @import tailwindcss with no @source directive, so Tailwind v4 auto-detects what to scan and ui/test is inside the scanned set... any occurrence of a utility name anywhere in those files emits the rule.\"\n\nThe first half is still true: index.css carries a bare @import \"tailwindcss\", there is no @source directive anywhere, and no tailwind config file. The second half is what I could not reproduce.\n\nWHAT I RAN, each a full build with the emitted stylesheet inspected:\n- A comment naming rotate-180 in ui/test/dom.test.mjs: no rule emitted, stylesheet byte-identical, same content hash.\n- The same comment in ui/src/lib/interaction.ts: no rule emitted.\n- The same comment in ui/src/components/TopBar.tsx: rule emitted.\n- A string literal \"rotate-180\" in TopBar.tsx: rule emitted, hash changed.\n\nThe last of those is the apparatus check, and it is the reason the first three mean anything. My first two runs produced no change and I nearly recorded \"the bug does not reproduce\" — but a negative result from an unvalidated build proves nothing, and the control showed the build does regenerate and does scan. Only then were the negatives evidence.\n\nWHAT IT MEANS. Prose in a .tsx file can still alter the shipped stylesheet, so the underlying worry is real. But the specific exposure the request names — test files under ui/test changing what users download — does not exist at this tree. Comments in .ts and .mjs were not scanned in any of my runs. The incident the request cites, a comment in a test file emitting a .transform rule, either involved a differently-typed file or predates a change in what Tailwind's auto-detection scans.\n\nSo the request as written would have me fix something that is not happening, and its stated condition — adding a utility name to a file under ui/test leaves the build byte-identical — already passes today. Implementing it would have produced a change with no observable effect and a test that could not fail.\n\nI am retiring it rather than reworking it. It is a Claude-to-Claude self-request, which is the shape AGENTS.md gives no standing to, and its premise is wrong; refiling the narrower true version as another self-request would repeat both errors. If the .tsx exposure is worth closing, it deserves its own assignment with the measurement above as its evidence, and an @source directive naming only the application sources is the obvious fix.\n\nOne filing error to record. Checking dependents before retiring, I typed an event id extended from the eight-character prefix and queried an event that does not exist, which returned \"no dependents\" for nothing at all. I caught it because the same command printed the resolved id beside my invented one, and re-ran the check against the real id: genuinely no dependents. That is the fifteenth time I have manufactured an identifier this way, and the first where the guard fired inside the same call rather than after the act."}